/* ===== The Seventeen — a research-grounded inquiry plan =====
   Source: "The Seventeen: A Research-Grounded Inquiry Plan" (Sept 2026).
   Structured, not rewritten. The instrument collects Beet's writing; it never
   generates answers. Grades: A multiple RCTs/meta · B ≥1 RCT/controlled ·
   C long use, no trials · S symbolic, prompt-generator only. */

var SEVENTEEN = {
  title: 'The Seventeen',
  sub: 'Not a set of answers. A path for thinking through seventeen questions, with the traditions, research and practices people have used on them, graded by the evidence behind each. The app holds answers; this instrument holds yours. A practice that maps to one already in the manual is the same practice under its research name: a pointer, not a copy. Log it in one place.',
  rules: [
    'Two practices live, never three. A third must replace one.',
    'The fourteen-day drop rule. A practice unused for fourteen days is dropped without comment. Not paused. Dropped. It can be re-adopted later as a fresh choice.',
    'One action per session. Not a plan. One if-then sentence.',
    'Write, do not think. Every prompt is answered on paper or screen. Thinking in the head is the escape hatch with better lighting.',
    'The routing rule. If a session produces material heavier than the container — flashbacks, sustained hopelessness, anything that frightens you about yourself — the session ends and Routing applies. Not failure. The practice being honest about its scope.'
  ],
  success: 'At twelve weeks, not seventeen answers. Four things: sixty to eighty pages in your own hand; a ledger of which weekly actions were kept, a record rather than a score; two or three practices that survived the drop rule by being used; and a shorter list of questions, some dissolved or merged, one or two become the real ones.',
  clusters: [
    { id: 'A', name: 'Legs', qs: [1, 4, 15], mech: 'Action, energy, and the loops that steal both', weeks: [1, 3] },
    { id: 'B', name: 'Ground', qs: [6, 7, 9, 13], mech: 'Regulation, the body, peace, and being seen', weeks: [4, 6] },
    { id: 'C', name: 'Self', qs: [5, 8, 10, 12], mech: 'Worth, freedom, security, identity', weeks: [7, 9] },
    { id: 'D', name: 'Depth', qs: [11, 14, 16], mech: 'Attention, needs, wounds', weeks: [10, 12] },
    { id: 'E', name: 'Lineage', qs: [2, 3, 17], mech: 'Masculinity, speech, inheritance — a parallel track, answered in company', weeks: [1, 12], parallel: true }
  ],
  method: [
    { id: 'sigh', name: 'The physiological floor', min: 5, grade: 'A', text: 'Five minutes of cyclic sighing: inhale through the nose, a second short inhale to top up, then a long exhale through the mouth. Balban, Spiegel, Huberman et al., Cell Reports Medicine 2023, about 110 participants over a month — greater mood improvement than an equal period of mindfulness meditation; no measured change in heart-rate variability. This is the Return-in-Place with a trial behind it. Do it first, always.', write: false },
    { id: 'terms', name: 'Define the terms', min: 5, grade: 'C', text: 'Before answering, write what you mean by the word doing hidden work in the question — in one sentence — then what you do not mean. Half the questions dissolve partially at this step. Epictetus opens the Enchiridion with a definition for exactly this reason.', write: true },
    { id: 'freewrite', name: 'Free-write', min: 20, grade: 'A', text: 'Twenty minutes, without stopping, without editing, no rereading until it is done. Pennebaker’s expressive-writing protocol, tested in well over a hundred studies since 1986: writing forces a structure onto experience that rumination never provides. Editing is locked. Backspace works inside the word you are on.', write: true, lock: true },
    { id: 'third', name: 'Read it back in the third person', min: 5, grade: 'B', text: 'Reread the free-write as if written by a man named Bate. Kross’s self-distancing research: “he” instead of “I” reduces rumination and reactivity while preserving insight. Write three sentences about what that man seems to believe. The third person has no defendant.', write: true, showPrev: 'freewrite' },
    { id: 'counter', name: 'Find the counter-evidence', min: 5, grade: 'A', text: 'Every belief that surfaced gets one line of counter-evidence, written down. Not to win the argument. A mind trained on the case for the prosecution needs to be made to file for the defense at least once. The core move of cognitive therapy.', write: true },
    { id: 'naikan', name: 'Naikan’s three questions', min: 10, grade: 'B', text: 'About the specific person this question touches: What did I receive from them? What did I give them? What troubles did I cause them? The fourth question — what troubles they caused me — is excluded, because everyone already asks it constantly. The exact counterweight to the case-builder.', write: true, optional: true },
    { id: 'action', name: 'One action, in if-then form', min: 2, grade: 'A', text: 'If [situation], then I [action]. Gollwitzer & Sheeran 2006, 94 studies, medium-to-large effect: the if-then hands the decision to the situation instead of to willpower. Name the trigger, not just the act. “I write for twenty minutes” is a wish. “If it is 6:40 and the coffee is poured, then I open the manuscript before the phone” is an implementation intention. This is the àfọ̀ṣẹ sentence with the trigger clause Gollwitzer’s work says matters.', write: true, ifthen: true }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, cluster: 'A', text: 'What if I stopped waiting for perfect, stopped being afraid of starting over, and moved?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Not “why don’t I act” — the mind already has ten answers — but “what mechanism converts an intention into an act without passing through the checkpoint called perfect.” An engineering question disguised as a courage question.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Gollwitzer’s implementation intentions. Oettingen’s mental contrasting (WOOP: Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan) — positive fantasy alone reduces effort; contrasting the wish with the obstacle increases it. Behavioral activation: action precedes motivation, not the reverse.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Morita therapy (Japan, 1920s): arugamama — accept the feeling as it is, then do the next necessary thing anyway. Hayes has acknowledged ACT’s debt to Morita. The Gita’s nishkama karma: action released from its fruit.'],
        ['History', 'The perfectionist start is a modern luxury. Craft traditions trained by apprenticeship: bad pots for years, in public, under a master — that was the curriculum. Beginning only when ready is roughly as old as learning alone from books.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Lamott’s “shitty first drafts” — the writers’ folk version of behavioral activation. Musashi: no fixed form; the practitioner who stops on a stance is cut there.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Implementation intentions: Gollwitzer & Sheeran 2006 meta-analysis, 94 studies, medium-to-large effect.', 'A'], ['Mental contrasting (WOOP): Oettingen, multiple RCTs; Rethinking Positive Thinking (2014).', 'A'], ['Behavioral activation: non-inferior to CBT for depression (Richards et al., Lancet 2016).', 'A'], ['Morita therapy: RCTs mostly in Japan and China; UK pilot (Sugg et al., BMJ Open 2018).', 'B']],
      prompts: ['Define “perfect” as you actually use it. What would have to be true before you allowed yourself to begin the book, the first invitation, the open mic? List the conditions. Mark each: real prerequisite, or permission slip?', 'Name the last three things you started imperfectly that went fine. Not big things. What was different about those starts?', 'WOOP the book, or whichever thing is waiting: the wish in one line; the best outcome in one line; the single internal obstacle that most reliably stops you; the if-then plan for that obstacle.', 'Naikan on yourself as a maker: what have you received from your unfinished work? What have you given it? What trouble have you caused it by waiting?', 'Reread in third person: what does Bate seem to believe happens if he starts before he is ready? Write it as a sentence. Then the counter-evidence.', 'Close with the if-then sentence for this week.'],
      practices: ['ifthen', 'fivemin', 'arugamama'],
      read: ['Oettingen, Rethinking Positive Thinking (2014)', 'Lamott, Bird by Bird (1994)', 'Reynolds, Constructive Living (1984) — the short American adaptation of Morita'],
      notes: [{"s": "Gollwitzer & Sheeran, implementation intentions (2006 meta-analysis)", "g": "Across 94 studies the plain form “if situation X, then I do Y” roughly doubled the odds of following through on a goal, and the effect held whether the goal was exercise, flossing, or completing a report. The mechanism they document: the if-then pre-loads the situation as a trigger, so the act fires without an in-the-moment decision. Willpower is never consulted.", "w": "The perfect-start checkpoint is a decision made at the worst possible time — the moment of beginning. The if-then removes the decision from that moment entirely. You choose on Sunday, in the cool; Tuesday at 6:40 simply executes."}, {"s": "Oettingen, Rethinking Positive Thinking (2014)", "g": "Twenty years of her lab’s studies show that vividly imagining a desired outcome actually lowers effort and blood pressure — the mind reads the fantasy as partly accomplished and relaxes. What works is mental contrasting: hold the wish, then name the specific internal obstacle, then plan for it. Her WOOP protocol (Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan) is the four-step version, and the Obstacle step is the one people skip.", "w": "WOOP the book: keep the dream, then name the obstacle, which for you is nearly always the same one — the first bad page."}, {"s": "Behavioral activation (Richards et al., Lancet 2016)", "g": "The COBRA trial gave 440 depressed adults either full CBT or behavioral activation — a much simpler treatment in which patients schedule and perform valued activities regardless of mood. Outcomes were equivalent at a year, and BA was cheaper to deliver. The theory underneath: mood follows action, not the reverse; waiting to feel ready is the disorder’s own maintenance loop.", "w": "Waiting for perfect is waiting to feel ready. The trial says the readiness arrives after the act, which means the five-minute start is not a trick — it is the treatment."}, {"s": "Morita therapy; Reynolds, Constructive Living (1984)", "g": "Shoma Morita’s 1920s Japanese psychiatry refused to treat feelings as the problem. His instruction, arugamama: accept the feeling exactly as it is, and do what needs doing anyway. Reynolds’s short American version distills it to three lines — accept your feelings, know your purpose, do what needs to be done. A UK pilot trial (Sugg et al. 2018) found it feasible and acceptable for depression; Steven Hayes has said ACT owes Morita a debt.", "w": "You have tried to clear the fear before starting. Morita says the clearing is the trap. Fear present, hand moving — both at once. That is the whole practice, and it is older than the therapy that later proved it."}, {"s": "Lamott, Bird by Bird (1994)", "g": "Her chapter on first drafts argues that every good writer she knows produces terrible first drafts on purpose, because the draft’s only job is to exist so a second one can fix it. She describes the perfectionist as someone who believes the first attempt must be presentable, and calls that belief the thing that keeps most people from writing at all.", "w": "The book you are not writing is waiting for a first draft you have forbidden yourself to write badly. Lamott’s permission is the same as Morita’s, from a desk instead of a clinic."}, {"s": "Musashi, The Book of Five Rings", "g": "The swordsman’s core teaching is that fixation on any stance, weapon or plan is where you get cut. He trained to have no preferred form so that nothing could be predicted or exploited, and he describes the mind that stops on a detail as already defeated.", "w": "The perfect start is a stance. Waiting for it is stopping on it. The Way section already named this tomaru; Musashi is the same diagnosis from the man who survived sixty duels."}],
      trap: 'Researching how to start. This question generates reading lists faster than any other, and reading is how the overflow refills. The rule for this question: no new book until the current if-then has been kept twice.' },

    { id: 4, cluster: 'A', text: 'How do I build present energy that builds up?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Two separate questions: how is energy deposited, and where is it leaking. Most people only work the first. Your own audit says the leaks are the bigger account.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Self-Determination Theory: vitality rises when autonomy, competence and relatedness are met; daily diaries show vitality tracks need satisfaction day to day. Sleep science: CBT-I and sleep regularity are strong; popular claims often outrun the data. Assertiveness training for the over-giving leak.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Chinese cultivation logic — qi follows attention; energy gathered by daily small deposit — and the Reservoir practice. Yoruba ìtutù: coolness as the condition under which force flows clean; energy spent in heat is wasted.'],
        ['History', 'Before electric light humans slept in two segments with a waking period between (Ekirch, At Day’s Close); the consolidated eight-hour block emerged with artificial lighting. For a man on four hours: the target is not a mythical eight, it is regularity and enough.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Nature exposure. White et al. 2019, nearly 20,000 people: 120 minutes a week in natural settings associated with significantly better wellbeing, no further gain past about 300. For a nature-element man this is not a metaphor.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Exercise for mood and energy: comparable to first-line treatment for mild-to-moderate depression.', 'A'], ['CBT-I for sleep: first-line, multiple meta-analyses.', 'A'], ['Nature exposure: White et al. 2019; Bratman et al. 2015 (ninety-minute nature walk reduced rumination).', 'A/B'], ['SDT need satisfaction and vitality: Reis et al. 2000, daily diary.', 'A'], ['Assertiveness training: RCTs since the 1970s; reliable effects on the ability to refuse.', 'A']],
      prompts: ['The last three times you said you were empty: the hour, what you had just done, and which tank was actually dry — body, attention, or feeling. Three entries before any definition.', 'The leak audit: for one week, at day’s end, name the single interaction or activity that cost the most and whether it was chosen or defaulted into. Seven entries. Read them in third person.', 'Write the three SDT needs as questions: where in my week am I autonomous, competent, connected? Which is starving?', 'The sleep honesty line: how many hours, really, and what is the story that makes four acceptable?', 'The generosity gate from the Money Protocol: the one “yes” this week that should have been a “no,” and what it cost.', 'If-then: one deposit (sleep anchor, green walk) or one leak closed (one refusal).'],
      practices: ['sleepanchor', 'greenhour', 'refusal'],
      read: ['Walker, Why We Sleep (2017) — mechanics; several strong claims challenged; motivation, not gospel', 'Williams, The Nature Fix (2017)', 'Pink, Drive (2009) — the concrete SDT popularization'],
      notes: [{"s": "Reis et al. 2000; Self-Determination Theory", "g": "A daily-diary study followed people for two weeks and found that on days when they felt more autonomous, competent and connected, they reported more vitality that same day — the three needs predicted energy day by day, independent of how much sleep or exercise they got. Deci and Ryan’s larger literature treats vitality as the felt sign that the three needs are being met.", "w": "Your energy question has three columns, and “tired” is the wrong word for a day in which you were competent but had no autonomy and no one of your own to talk to. The leak audit tells you which column is empty."}, {"s": "CBT-I (cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia)", "g": "The first-line treatment for insomnia across every major guideline, ahead of medication. Its opening move is not more sleep but regularity: a fixed wake time seven days a week, which anchors the circadian rhythm so that sleep pressure builds predictably. Meta-analyses show durable improvement without drugs.", "w": "You are on four hours with a variable wake time. CBT-I says fix the wake time first and let the rest follow. One anchor, not a protocol."}, {"s": "Ekirch, At Day’s Close (2005)", "g": "A historian’s survey of pre-industrial European records found routine references to a “first sleep” and “second sleep” with a waking hour between — used for prayer, sex, conversation, or lying quietly. The consolidated eight-hour block emerged with artificial lighting. His point is not that segmented sleep is better, but that the eight-hour ideal is a recent norm, not a biological law.", "w": "Loosen the guilt about the mythical eight. The target is regularity and enough, and “enough” is more than four."}, {"s": "White et al. 2019, Scientific Reports; Bratman et al. 2015, PNAS", "g": "White’s team analysed nearly 20,000 people’s self-reports and found a threshold: those who spent 120 minutes or more per week in natural settings were significantly more likely to report good health and high wellbeing, and the benefit plateaued around 200–300 minutes. Bratman’s lab study sent people on a ninety-minute walk through either grassland or a busy road: the nature walkers reported less rumination and showed reduced activity in the subgenual prefrontal cortex, the region that lights up during self-focused brooding.", "w": "For a nature-element man this is the cheapest deposit available and the one the season will steal first. Two hours a week, in twenty-minute pieces, is the dose."}, {"s": "Assertiveness training research", "g": "Trials since the 1970s consistently show that refusing is a trainable skill: structured practice at saying no raises the ability to refuse and lowers the distress that follows. The guilt after a refusal is treated as an expected withdrawal symptom of a people-pleasing pattern, not as a signal that the refusal was wrong.", "w": "Your values audit found over-giving in all four values. Assertiveness training says the guilt after the first refusal is the pattern losing, and the second refusal is easier."}, {"s": "Pink, Drive (2009)", "g": "The readable popularization of Self-Determination Theory: he argues the carrot-and-stick model of motivation is wrong for most modern work, and that autonomy, mastery and purpose (his renaming of the three needs) are what sustain effort. The book is thin on practice but clear on the mechanism.", "w": "Read it instead of the academic SDT papers. It gives you the vocabulary for the three questions in the thinking path."}],
      trap: 'Optimizing intake while the leaks run. You will be tempted to build the perfect sleep protocol and keep saying yes to everyone. The leak side is where your energy actually goes.' },

    { id: 15, cluster: 'A', text: 'What are my addictions?', person: false,
      reframe: 'What am I using to escape a feeling I have not named, and what would the feeling be if the escape were closed. Brewer’s definition: a repeated behavior despite adverse consequences, driven by a loop of trigger, behavior, reward.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Brewer’s habit-loop model and RAIN adapted for craving; Marlatt’s urge surfing; Lembke’s dopamine-balance model — why the phone reach feels like relief and produces deficit.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Buddhist taṇhā as the engine of suffering. Somé: every addiction is a displaced ceremony — the cure is restoring the rite, not white-knuckling abstinence. The Anti-Performance Clause already names comprehension itself as one of your escapes.'],
        ['History', 'The attention economy is engineered. The phone reach is a design success more than a personal failing (Harris, Newport). Knowing that does not remove the pull; it removes the shame, which is part of the loop.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Maté: not why the addiction, but why the pain. Newport’s thirty-day digital declutter as an experiment, not a purity test.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Mindfulness training for smoking cessation (Brewer et al. 2011 RCT); app-based craving programs with RCT support.', 'B'], ['Urge surfing / relapse prevention (Marlatt): decades of controlled research.', 'A'], ['Digital friction (timers, grayscale, notification removal): modest but real reductions.', 'B'], ['Somé’s displaced ceremony: traditional claim.', 'C']],
      prompts: ['List every repeated behavior you do despite not wanting to. Phone, the comprehension loop, irony, over-giving, staying up, checking her status. No judgment; the list.', 'For each: the trigger (what feeling arrives just before), the behavior, the reward (the first thirty seconds). Write the loop out. Seeing it clearly is itself the intervention.', 'For the phone: for three days, before every reach, write one word for what you were feeling. Read the words. That list is the real addiction.', 'Naikan on the phone: what have you received from it? Given? What trouble have you caused — to sleep, to Marie, to the book?', 'Somé’s question: what ceremony is each addiction impersonating? Comprehension impersonates initiation. The phone impersonates contact. Over-giving impersonates belonging. Write the ceremony each stands in for.', 'If-then: one loop, one substitute trigger for the week.'],
      practices: ['urgesurf', 'oneword', 'cutoff'],
      read: ['Brewer, Unwinding Anxiety (2021)', 'Lembke, Dopamine Nation (2021)', 'Newport, Digital Minimalism (2019)', 'Maté, In the Realm of Hungry Ghosts (2008) — first third if time is short'],
      notes: [{"s": "Brewer, Unwinding Anxiety (2021)", "g": "Brewer, a psychiatrist and neuroscientist, maps every habit — smoking, worry, doom-scrolling — onto one loop: trigger, behavior, reward. His clinical finding is that people cannot think their way out of a loop but can become disenchanted with it: when you pay close attention to how the reward actually feels in the moment, the brain updates its reward value, and the pull weakens. His 2011 smoking trial found mindfulness training beat the standard American Lung Association program, and his app-based programs have since shown effects in randomized trials.", "w": "You do not need to fight the phone. You need to feel, honestly, what the first thirty seconds of the reach give you. His research says the feeling is usually less than the craving promised, and noticing that is the intervention."}, {"s": "Marlatt, urge surfing / relapse prevention", "g": "Alan Marlatt’s relapse-prevention model, tested over decades, treats an urge as a wave that peaks and passes in a minute or two if not fed or fought. The practice: notice where the urge sits in the body, breathe, and watch it rise and fall. The belief that urges last until satisfied is what makes them last.", "w": "The reach for the phone at 11pm feels like a command. Marlatt says it is a ninety-second wave. Ride one and you know."}, {"s": "Lembke, Dopamine Nation (2021)", "g": "A Stanford addiction psychiatrist’s account of the pleasure-pain balance: every dopamine hit is followed by a compensating dip, and repeated hits tilt the baseline downward, so the phone that once gave relief now only returns you to zero. Her clinical advice is a dopamine fast — thirty days off the behavior — after which the baseline resets and ordinary things feel good again.", "w": "The phone at night is not giving you anything. It is briefly cancelling a deficit it created. Her thirty-day reset is a structured experiment, not a purity test."}, {"s": "Somé, on addiction as displaced ceremony", "g": "Malidoma Somé argued that Western addictions are ritual hunger with nowhere to go: the psyche needs initiation, grief, ecstasy and belonging, and when the rites that supplied them are gone, substances and screens step in as counterfeits. His remedy is not abstinence but restoring the rite the addiction is impersonating.", "w": "The comprehension loop impersonates initiation. The phone impersonates contact. Over-giving impersonates belonging. Name the ceremony and you know what to build instead of what to forbid."}, {"s": "Maté, In the Realm of Hungry Ghosts (2008)", "g": "From years in Vancouver’s Downtown Eastside, Maté argues that addiction is never about the substance and always about pain — the question is not why the addiction but why the pain. He includes his own compulsive classical-music purchasing to show that the mechanism runs at every scale, not only in the addicts society names.", "w": "Read the first third for the reframe. The pain under your loops has names already in this manual — not chosen, not enough, not seen — and the loops are the anaesthetic."}, {"s": "Newport, Digital Minimalism (2019)", "g": "Newport proposes a thirty-day declutter: remove every optional technology, then reintroduce only what serves a value you can name, with rules for when and how. He documents the attention economy’s design intent — engagement engineered by teams who know the psychology — and argues that individual willpower is the wrong tool against it.", "w": "Knowing the phone is engineered removes the shame, and the shame is part of the loop. His declutter is the practical version of Lembke’s reset."}],
      trap: 'Treating comprehension as exempt. The phone is the addiction you will admit to; the loop of reading and framework-building is the one that costs more, and this document can feed it. Apply the one-word log to the urge to open one more source.' },

    { id: 6, cluster: 'B', text: 'What do I do daily to choose my peace, to live it, and protect it?', person: false,
      reframe: 'What is the minimum daily architecture that produces a regulated nervous system, and what defends it. Three verbs, three different tools.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Morning-routine research is thin; the defensible observation is that the first input sets attentional tone. Assertiveness training for “protect.” Mindfulness-based programs for “live.”'],
        ['Tradition', 'The Ignatian examen: five-step evening review used daily for four hundred years. Islamic muhasaba: the daily reckoning, recommended by al-Ghazali. Seneca examining his day each night (On Anger 3.36). Three traditions arrived independently at one instrument: a short structured evening review.'],
        ['History', 'The morning practices in the Grammar of Return sit inside a global pattern: nearly every tradition places its anchor at the first waking minutes, before speech.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Stoic Week experiments (LeBon et al., annual since 2012) report wellbeing gains from a week of Stoic practices; pre-post and self-selected, so graded low.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Mindfulness-based programs: large meta-analyses, moderate effects on stress and anxiety.', 'A'], ['Assertiveness training.', 'A'], ['Structured evening review (examen, muhasaba, Stoic): centuries of daily use; no trials.', 'C'], ['Sleep regularity as a peace variable.', 'A']],
      prompts: ['Write what disturbed your peace most often in the last seven days, one entry per day. The disturbances are the data; peace is their absence, not a state.', '“Choose”: what is the first input of your day, honestly? Write the last seven mornings.', '“Live”: which hours of the week are you not building a case, not performing, not managing anyone? Name them. Peace already lives there.', '“Protect”: whose access to you is unlimited, and who has earned that? The list.', 'Rosenberg’s rewrite: every “I have to” from this week restated as “I choose to, because.” Which cannot be honestly restated? Examine those.', 'If-then: one morning anchor or one evening review, for the week.'],
      practices: ['morningminute', 'eveningreview', 'refusal'],
      read: ['Pigliucci, How to Be a Stoic (2017)', 'Martin, The Jesuit Guide to (Almost) Everything (2010) — the examen, for non-Catholics', 'Rosenberg, Nonviolent Communication — in the manual'],
      notes: [{"s": "The Ignatian examen; Martin, The Jesuit Guide to (Almost) Everything (2010)", "g": "Ignatius of Loyola prescribed a five-step evening review in the 1500s and it has been practised daily by Jesuits and lay people since: give thanks; ask for light; review the day hour by hour; notice where you moved toward or away from what matters; look to tomorrow. Martin’s book presents it for people of any or no faith and insists it takes five minutes, not an hour.", "w": "You are looking for the daily architecture of peace. Four hundred years of daily use say the evening review is a load-bearing wall, and the Zengzi Close in The Way is already a version of it."}, {"s": "Islamic muhasaba; al-Ghazali", "g": "Al-Ghazali, the eleventh-century theologian, taught daily self-accounting: at night, review the day’s actions as a merchant reviews his ledger — what was gained, what was lost, what must be corrected tomorrow. The word means reckoning, and it is a duty, not an option, in the Sufi ethical tradition.", "w": "Three traditions arrived at the same instrument from different directions. When Jesuits, Sufis and Stoics independently build the same tool, the tool is worth having."}, {"s": "Seneca, On Anger 3.36", "g": "Seneca describes his own nightly practice: when the lamp is out and his wife has gone quiet, he goes over the whole day, hiding nothing from himself, and asks what fault he cured, what he resisted, where he was better. He says he sleeps well afterward because the review has an ending.", "w": "The Stoic version is the shortest. The point he makes that the others assume: the review is closed. It does not run all night. Maintenance, not litigation."}, {"s": "Mindfulness-based programs (MBSR, MBCT)", "g": "Large meta-analyses of mindfulness-based stress reduction and cognitive therapy show moderate, reliable reductions in stress, anxiety and depressive relapse. The mechanism most supported is decentering — learning to watch a thought as an event rather than obey it as a fact.", "w": "“Live” peace is not a state you achieve; it is the capacity to have a bad thought without building a case on it. That capacity is trainable and the training is short."}, {"s": "Pigliucci, How to Be a Stoic (2017)", "g": "A philosopher’s practical guide organised around Epictetus: the dichotomy of control (what is up to us and what is not), the discipline of desire, the evening review. Concrete, modern, and light on metaphysics.", "w": "For “protect”: the dichotomy of control is the oldest boundary tool in the West. What is not up to you — her weather, the recruiter’s silence — is not yours to carry, and peace is what is left when you set it down."}],
      trap: 'Building a routine so complete it becomes the performance. The morning minute is sixty seconds, not a ceremony. Past ten minutes it has become something to be good at.' },

    { id: 7, cluster: 'B', text: 'What is my peace? Where is my peace?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Whether peace is a place, a condition, or an absence — and whether the quiet you sometimes find is peace or withdrawal.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Chödrön’s groundlessness has a clinical cousin in ACT’s acceptance: willingness to have the present experience, not removal of the unwanted. Secure people report a portable felt safety, not a location (Mikulincer & Shaver).'],
        ['Tradition', 'Ìtutù: coolness as climate. Sufi sakīna: tranquility that descends rather than is manufactured. Quaker expectant waiting: the silence after the noise stops, not before.'],
        ['History', 'The eightfold fence: the Japanese inner chamber as sanctuary or prison depending on whether the door opens. Your Aloof drama makes this the central distinction.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'The interruptibility test: real peace survives being interrupted by someone who loves you; the counterfeit needs you unreachable. Not from a study. From the manual, and the most useful single test on the question.']
      ],
      evidence: [['ACT acceptance processes: multiple meta-analyses.', 'A'], ['Attachment security and felt safety.', 'A'], ['Nature exposure and rumination: Bratman et al. 2015.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['Define peace. Then three moments in the last month where you had it. Where, who was there, what was your body doing?', 'Three moments of quiet that were not peace — the fence, the withdrawal, the composed silence. What distinguishes the two sets?', 'The interruptibility test on each: if Marie had called into that moment, would it have held or broken? The moments that held are where your peace actually lives, whatever you believe about it — write the address.', 'Write where your peace is not: the conditions you are waiting on — job, debt, ocean. Then one sentence on what you would do if they never resolved.','If-then: one visit to a place on the list, this week.'],
      practices: ['peacemap', 'sigh'],
      read: ['Chödrön, When Things Fall Apart — in the manual', 'Harris, The Happiness Trap (2007) — the concrete ACT book'],
      notes: [{"s": "Harris, The Happiness Trap (2007); ACT acceptance", "g": "Harris’s popular ACT manual argues that the pursuit of feeling good is itself the trap: struggling against unwanted feelings amplifies them. Acceptance in ACT means willingness to have the present experience while acting on values — not resignation. Meta-analyses of ACT show reliable effects across anxiety, depression and chronic pain.", "w": "Peace conditioned on the debt clearing and the ocean closing is the happiness trap in your own vocabulary. Willingness to have this year as it is, while acting on your values inside it, is what ACT calls peace."}, {"s": "Mikulincer & Shaver, attachment security", "g": "Decades of studies find that securely attached adults carry a portable felt sense of safety — a background assurance that support exists — which lets them explore, tolerate distress and recover faster. It is not tied to place or to one person’s presence.", "w": "If your peace only exists in one apartment, one call, or one weekend, it is a location, not a security. The felt sense travels; the fence does not."}, {"s": "Bratman et al. 2015", "g": "The ninety-minute nature walk reduced self-reported rumination and lowered activity in the brain region associated with brooding, compared with an urban walk of equal length.", "w": "Where is your peace: green, water, off pavement. The scan agrees with your element."}, {"s": "Chödrön, When Things Fall Apart (1997)", "g": "Chödrön teaches that groundlessness is the actual condition and the search for solid ground is what produces suffering; peace is found by staying with the uncertainty rather than fleeing it. Her instruction is to lean in to the moment things fall apart.", "w": "The peace that survives the conditions never resolving is the only peace you will hold for years. She is describing it from a Buddhist monastery; ACT describes it from a clinic; same instrument."}],
      trap: 'Mistaking the fence for the sanctuary. The Aloof reports withdrawal as peace, and it feels identical from inside. Only the interruptibility test tells them apart.' },

    { id: 9, cluster: 'B', text: 'What is my body unified with at this moment?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Not a question to answer; an instrument. Its function is to drop attention from the head into the body, and its value is entirely in the asking. The research name for what it trains is interoception.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Interoception (Craig; Khalsa; Garfinkel): accuracy in sensing internal states predicts emotional regulation, and it is trainable. Body scan from MBSR. Somatic Experiencing (Levine); van der Kolk.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Ẹ̀mí, the breath, as the only present-tense component of the person. Vipassana body-sweeping. The lower dantian. Yoga as interoceptive training, which is what it was before it became fitness.'],
        ['History', 'Western thought divorced body from mind around Descartes and has been remarrying them since the 1990s. The traditions never divorced them.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Gendlin’s Focusing: attend to the felt sense of a situation in the body and wait for a word or image to arrive. Developed from research on what made therapy work.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Body scan and MBSR for stress; interoceptive accuracy improves with practice.', 'A/B'], ['Somatic Experiencing: one RCT for PTSD (Brom et al. 2017).', 'B'], ['Trauma-sensitive yoga: RCT (van der Kolk et al. 2014).', 'B'], ['Focusing: several small controlled studies.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['Do not write about this question. Ask it hourly for one day and each time write only the body part that answers first. Jaw, chest, gut, shoulders, feet.', 'At day’s end, read the words. What does the pattern say about where you hold the day?', 'The honest inventory: sleep hours, the ankles and knees, breath rate when working. Written as a mechanic writes a vehicle report.', 'Gendlin’s question, once: “what is the whole feel of my life right now, in the body?” Wait. Do not supply an answer. Write whatever arrives, including nothing.', 'If-then: one body deposit for the week — the rehab you deferred, a walk, a stretch.'],
      practices: ['hourlydrop', 'focusing', 'rehab'],
      read: ['Gendlin, Focusing (1978)', 'Van der Kolk, The Body Keeps the Score (2014) — trauma claims sometimes broader than the evidence', 'Levine, Waking the Tiger (1997)'],
      notes: [{"s": "Interoception research (Craig; Khalsa; Garfinkel)", "g": "Interoception is the sensing of internal bodily states — heartbeat, breath, gut, muscle tension. Studies find that people who sense these more accurately regulate emotion better and are less prone to anxiety, and that accuracy improves with practice such as body scanning. Garfinkel distinguishes accuracy from the confidence you have in it; the gap between the two predicts anxiety.", "w": "Your question is an interoception exercise with a philosophical name. Asked hourly, one word, it trains the accuracy Garfinkel’s work links to regulation of the wave."}, {"s": "Gendlin, Focusing (1978)", "g": "Gendlin studied recordings of therapy and found that clients who improved shared one behaviour: they paused, attended to a vague bodily sense of the problem, and waited for words to arrive from it rather than about it. He wrote a six-step method to teach that: clear a space; feel the whole problem in the body; find a handle word or image; check it against the felt sense; ask what it needs; receive. Small controlled studies support it.", "w": "Your instinct to answer this question with a concept is exactly what Focusing forbids. The felt sense comes first and the word second, or the exercise has not happened."}, {"s": "Van der Kolk, The Body Keeps the Score (2014)", "g": "The argument is that trauma is stored as bodily state, not as narrative, and that talk alone does not reach it; body-based approaches — yoga, movement, breath — do. His 2014 randomized trial found trauma-sensitive yoga reduced PTSD symptoms. Some of the book’s broader neuroscience claims have been challenged; the clinical direction has held.", "w": "Four hours of sleep and a braced breath are the body keeping a score you have not read. The ankles and knees are on the same ledger."}, {"s": "Levine, Waking the Tiger (1997); Brom et al. 2017", "g": "Levine observed that wild animals discharge threat through shaking and movement after escape and proposed that human trauma is the discharge interrupted. Somatic Experiencing works by slowly completing the interrupted bodily response. Brom’s 2017 randomized trial found it reduced PTSD symptoms versus waitlist.", "w": "The braced body is a response that never finished. The rehab, the walk, the shake — these are completions, not fitness."}],
      trap: 'Answering with a concept. “My body is unified with my purpose” is the escape hatch in somatic clothing. The only valid answers are body parts and sensations.' },

    { id: 13, cluster: 'B', text: 'How do I breathe through fear and not feeling seen?', person: true,
      reframe: 'Two questions in one sentence. Fear has a physiological answer. Not being seen has a behavioral one — the harder of the two, because being seen requires the exact exposure the Aloof drama exists to prevent.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Fear: the exposure literature — graded, repeated exposure reduces the fear; the single most robust finding in clinical psychology. Being seen: Brown’s vulnerability research; graded visibility as an exposure hierarchy.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Baldwin’s masks. Lorde: your silence will not protect you. The orí address — speaking aloud to oneself as the first act of being seen by oneself.'],
        ['History', 'Pre-performance rituals reduce anxiety and improve performance even when the performer knows they are arbitrary (Brooks et al. 2016). Every culture of performers has them.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Improv for social anxiety: Felsman et al. 2019, 2020. Toastmasters: no trials, decades of structured graded exposure to being seen and heard.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Exposure therapy: the strongest evidence base in the field.', 'A'], ['Cyclic sighing for acute fear.', 'A'], ['Pre-performance rituals: Brooks et al. 2016.', 'B'], ['Improv for social anxiety: Felsman et al.', 'B'], ['Brown’s vulnerability research: qualitative.', 'C']],
      prompts: ['Separate the two. “Fear”: list what you fear this month. “Unseen”: list where you feel unseen. Different lists, different tools.', 'For fear: for each item, what would happen if it came true, then what you would do the next day. Seneca’s premeditatio; decatastrophizing in CBT.', 'For unseen: where have you chosen not to be visible? The composed summary, the polished ask, the feeling processed offstage. Then: who has seen you unpolished in the last month? If nobody, the loop is closed at your end.', 'Build the exposure ladder for being seen: ten rungs from easiest (one unedited voice note to Marie) to hardest (the open mic). Order them.', 'Write the sentence you would say if the mask were off, to one named person. The sentence itself, not a description of it.','If-then: rung one of the ladder, this week.'],
      practices: ['ladder', 'preritual', 'improv'],
      read: ['Brown, Daring Greatly (2012)', 'Ferriss, fear-setting (TED 2017); Seneca, Letters 13 and 24'],
      notes: [{"s": "Exposure therapy", "g": "The most replicated finding in clinical psychology: graded, repeated contact with the feared thing, without escape, reduces fear — because the predicted catastrophe fails to arrive and the brain learns it. The key variables are the gradient (small steps), repetition, and not fleeing before the fear peaks and falls.", "w": "Being seen is the feared thing, and the Aloof drama is the escape response that keeps the fear alive. The visibility ladder is exposure with your own rungs."}, {"s": "Brooks et al. 2016, rituals and performance", "g": "Across several experiments, people who performed a short arbitrary ritual before a stressful task — a maths test, singing in public — reported less anxiety and performed better than those who did not, even when told the ritual was made up. The ritual works through felt control, not belief.", "w": "Every griot, actor and athlete has a pre-performance sequence. Yours: two long exhales, feet on the floor, one line. Same before every rung. Arbitrary is fine."}, {"s": "Felsman et al. 2019, 2020, improv and social anxiety", "g": "Adolescents in a school improv program showed reduced social anxiety and increased tolerance of uncertainty compared with controls; later work extended it. The mechanism proposed: improv is graded exposure to being watched while making mistakes, with the rule that mistakes are gifts.", "w": "One class, once, as a rung. Not to join. To find out what being seen unpolished costs when the room is built for it."}, {"s": "Brown, Daring Greatly (2012)", "g": "From grounded-theory interviews with thousands of people, Brown concludes that vulnerability — uncertainty, risk, emotional exposure — is the birthplace of connection, and that shame’s survival depends on secrecy, silence and judgment. Her research is qualitative, widely used, and she names the armor people wear: perfectionism, numbing, foreboding joy.", "w": "The composed summary sent instead of the weather is armor she would recognise. Her finding: the mask does not protect the connection; it starves it."}, {"s": "Seneca, Letters 13 and 24; fear-setting", "g": "Seneca argues that we suffer more in imagination than in reality and prescribes premeditatio: define the feared outcome precisely, ask what you would actually do if it came, and notice that you could bear it. Tim Ferriss’s fear-setting exercise is the same in worksheet form.", "w": "Name the fear, write what happens if it comes true, write the next day. Most of your fears do not survive being written down in full."}],
      trap: 'Treating “not seen” as something done to you. The loop closes at your end: the mask goes on, then the ache arrives as if from outside. No amount of breathing fixes a loop you hold both ends of. Only the ladder does.' },

    { id: 5, cluster: 'C', text: 'Am I enough?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Enough for what, measured by whom. Unanswerable as posed because it names neither. The useful version is “am I congruent,” and the useful practice is self-compassion — more trial evidence than almost anything else here.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Neff’s self-compassion: self-kindness, common humanity, mindfulness — measurable and trainable. Mindful Self-Compassion has RCT support; Ferrari et al. 2019 meta-analysis, 27 RCTs, moderate effects. Kross’s self-distancing for the internal tribunal.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Mussar’s anavah: occupying no more than your place and no less than your space. “Am I enough” is a humility disorder in that sense — it assigns you less than your space. Muhasaba runs the review without the verdict. The Yoruba answer: you chose this orí; enoughness was settled before the crossing.'],
        ['History', 'The internalized gaze. Fanon on how a colonial standard installs itself as the inner judge; hooks on boys learning worth as function. The question has a genealogy, and it is not personal.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Goggins’ cookie jar: a written file of evidence of past capacity, consulted when the tribunal convenes. A practitioner’s version of the CBT positive data log, which has trial support for low self-esteem.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Self-compassion interventions: Ferrari et al. 2019, 27 RCTs.', 'A'], ['Self-distancing: Kross.', 'B'], ['Positive data log (Fennell’s low self-esteem CBT).', 'A/B'], ['Mussar: centuries of practice.', 'C']],
      prompts: ['Complete the sentence, honestly: “I would be enough if ______.” Ten times. For each, write who set that standard. Name them.', 'The last three times the question fired. What was happening thirty seconds before? The trigger is more informative than the question.', 'Neff’s exercise: write what you would say to a man you respect who came to you with this exact question. Read it addressed to yourself, in the third person.', 'The congruence swap: replace “am I enough” with “is what I said this week what I did this week.” Answer that instead. It has an answer.', 'Mussar’s frame: where this month did you take less than your space? More than your place? Both are the same disorder.', 'If-then: one entry in the evidence file this week, in the oríkì format — the name, and the moment that earned it.'],
      practices: ['selfcompassion', 'evidencefile', 'rename'],
      read: ['Neff, Self-Compassion (2011)', 'Morinis, Everyday Holiness (2007)', 'Kross, Chatter (2021)'],
      notes: [{"s": "Neff, Self-Compassion (2011); Ferrari et al. 2019", "g": "Neff defines self-compassion as three parts: kindness toward yourself in failure, recognising failure as common to all humans, and holding the pain mindfully rather than over-identifying with it. Her research finds it more strongly linked to wellbeing than self-esteem and free of self-esteem’s downsides — no need to be above average. The 2019 meta-analysis of 27 randomized trials found moderate improvements in depression, anxiety, self-criticism and wellbeing.", "w": "“Am I enough” is a self-esteem question, and self-esteem needs a ranking. Self-compassion needs none. The break — hand on chest, three sentences — is the fastest tested route out of the tribunal."}, {"s": "Kross, Chatter (2021)", "g": "Kross’s lab found that people who reflected on painful experiences using their own name or “you” instead of “I” — distanced self-talk — showed less emotional reactivity, less rumination, and better performance under stress, with the shift visible in brain activity within a second. His book collects the tools: distanced self-talk, time travel, ritual, ordering the environment.", "w": "“Bate is asking whether he is enough again” is a sentence with no defendant in it. Say it in the third person and the trial loses its prosecutor."}, {"s": "Fennell, positive data log (CBT for low self-esteem)", "g": "Melanie Fennell’s treatment for low self-esteem includes keeping a written log of evidence that contradicts the core belief — small acts, competence, kindness received — because the belief filters such evidence out automatically. Trials of her protocol show improvement in self-esteem and depression.", "w": "Your oríkì file is a positive data log with a Yoruba name. The rule that entries must be earned is what makes it evidence rather than affirmation."}, {"s": "Morinis, Everyday Holiness (2007); Mussar", "g": "Mussar is a Jewish practice of character refinement through daily attention to traits. Morinis presents humility (anavah) as the first trait, defined by Rabbi Simcha Zissel as occupying no more than your place and no less than your space — humility disorders run in both directions, and taking too little space is one of them.", "w": "“Am I enough” assigns you less than your space. In Mussar’s frame it is not modesty; it is the same disorder as arrogance, pointed inward."}, {"s": "Fanon, Black Skin, White Masks (1952); hooks, The Will to Change (2004)", "g": "Fanon describes how a colonial standard is internalised until the colonised man judges himself through the coloniser’s eyes and can never pass. hooks argues that boys are taught worth as function — you are what you provide and perform — so a man’s resting state becomes insufficiency awaiting proof.", "w": "The question has a genealogy. It was installed, and it was designed to keep you auditioning. Knowing that does not answer it; it lets you stop taking the call."}],
      trap: 'Answering it. Every answer, including yes, keeps the tribunal in session. Notice the question has fired and go keep a word.' },

    { id: 8, cluster: 'C', text: 'How do I feel free in myself?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Free from what, and free for what. The first half has a psychological answer (the audience). The second has a values answer (what you would do with it).',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'ACT’s psychological flexibility: acting on values in the presence of difficult internal experience, not in its absence. Values clarification as the “free for.” Behavioral experiments for testing whether the imagined audience reacts as feared.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Frankl’s last freedom. Fanon’s disalienation: refusing the frame, not rebelling within it. The Stoic dichotomy of control. Acting from the inner head rather than the fortified position is what freedom looks like from inside.'],
        ['History', 'Black freedom thought (Baldwin, Fanon, Diop) is in the manual; the through-line is that freedom is the refusal of an installed standard, and the installation is historical, not personal.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Stanislavski’s public solitude: the capacity to be private in public — the exact skill the Aloof man lacks.']
      ],
      evidence: [['ACT: meta-analyses across conditions.', 'A'], ['Values clarification: RCTs within ACT; values card sort widely used.', 'A/B'], ['Behavioral experiments: core CBT method.', 'A']],
      prompts: ['List what you are not free from this month, concretely — the debt, the paperwork, the call you owe, the sentence you cannot send. Freedom is whatever the list leaves out.', 'The audience inventory: whose approval are you unconsciously working for? Parents, the imagined gatekeeper, the Bakwa reader, the man who left Douala, Marie. Which of those juries has actually adjourned?', 'Values sort: from your eight North Star lines, the three that matter most. One action this month that served each. One that betrayed each.', 'The behavioral experiment: one thing you would do if no one were watching. Predict the reaction. Do it. Record the actual. Freedom is usually the gap.', 'Fanon’s question: which standard are you measuring yourself against, and who set it? One sentence. Then whether you would choose it.', 'If-then: the experiment, this week.'],
      practices: ['valuessort', 'withoutnod', 'publicsolitude'],
      read: ['Frankl, Man’s Search for Meaning — in the manual', 'Harris, The Happiness Trap', 'Fanon via the manual’s rendering'],
      notes: [{"s": "ACT, psychological flexibility (Hayes)", "g": "ACT defines its goal not as feeling better but as psychological flexibility: the ability to act on chosen values while difficult thoughts and feelings are present. Meta-analyses across dozens of conditions support it. Values clarification — often a card sort — is the step that makes “free for” concrete.", "w": "Free is not the absence of the audience in your head. It is acting on your values while the audience heckles. That is measurable in weekly acts, not in a feeling."}, {"s": "Frankl, Man’s Search for Meaning (1946)", "g": "From Auschwitz, Frankl concludes that everything can be taken from a man but the last freedom — to choose one’s attitude in any set of circumstances. The book’s second half outlines logotherapy: meaning as the primary human drive, found through work, love, or the stance taken toward unavoidable suffering.", "w": "By the definition of freedom as absence of constraint, you will not be free this year. By Frankl’s, you can be free by Tuesday."}, {"s": "Fanon, disalienation", "g": "Fanon’s exit from the internalised colonial gaze is not rebellion — which still takes the coloniser as reference — but disalienation: refusing the frame in which one’s acceptability was ever the question. The move is to stop measuring rather than to score higher.", "w": "Doing the opposite of what the audience wants is still performing for it. Fanon names the third option: leave the theatre."}, {"s": "Epictetus, Enchiridion 1", "g": "The handbook opens: some things are up to us — opinion, desire, action — and some are not — body, reputation, office. Confusing the two is the source of every disturbance. Freedom is the practice of caring only about the first category.", "w": "Whose nod you are working for belongs in the second category. Their verdict is not up to you, and Epictetus says that is the news that sets you free."}, {"s": "Stanislavski, public solitude", "g": "The acting teacher trained performers to draw a small circle of attention around a task and become fully absorbed in it on stage, in front of an audience — public solitude. The skill is being private in public, not by hiding but by attending.", "w": "The Aloof man is private in public by hiding. The actor is private in public by attending. Same result on the surface, opposite mechanism, and only one of them is free."}],
      trap: 'Rebellion. Doing the opposite of what the audience wants is still performing for the audience. Freedom is indifference to the verdict, not inversion of it.' },

    { id: 10, cluster: 'C', text: 'What does it mean to be secure?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Which security: financial (arithmetic), or internal (the felt sense of being permitted to exist). Different materials, and the belief that the first delivers the second is the trap.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Attachment security as a portable felt safety, and the finding that it can be primed — temporarily increased by recalling being safely held. Thaler & Benartzi’s Save More Tomorrow (2004) for the arithmetic.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Diop’s restored self-knowledge as the security no institution can revoke. Ubuntu: security as embeddedness, not fortification. Orí as the security settled before birth.'],
        ['History', 'The breadwinner script makes provision the whole of male worth, which is why financial insecurity lands on men as existential insecurity.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Housel: security as the gap between what you have and what you need, with “need” the variable most never examine. The Tao’s enough line with charts.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Attachment security priming: multiple lab studies.', 'B'], ['Automated saving: field studies.', 'A'], ['Emergency fund and financial stress: large survey literature; causal evidence modest.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['Two columns: what would make you secure in money, and what would make you feel secure. Fill both before reading either. They will not match.', 'The money version is in the Money Protocol. Which number are you actually working toward this month, and did the generosity gate hold?', 'The felt version: three times in your life you felt completely safe with another person. Where, who, what was happening. Recalling these increases felt security for hours.', 'The trap test: imagine the floor achieved. Debt zero, savings held. What does the tribunal say then? If the same thing, the money was never the variable.', 'Ubuntu’s question: who holds you? The names. If the list is one name nine thousand kilometers away, that is the security gap, and it is not financial.', 'If-then: one automation (money) or one holding (a call to one of the names), this week.'],
      practices: ['automation', 'securityprime', 'holdingcall'],
      read: ['Housel, The Psychology of Money (2020)', 'Levine & Heller, Attached — in the manual', 'Sethi, I Will Teach You to Be Rich (2019) — automation mechanics; ignore the title'],
      notes: [{"s": "Mikulincer & Shaver, security priming", "g": "In lab studies, briefly recalling or being shown cues of a secure relationship — a memory of being cared for, a name, an image — temporarily raises felt security and, downstream, compassion, openness and tolerance of threat. The effect is short but reliable and repeatable.", "w": "Two minutes recalling one of the three safe moments is a tested intervention, not a nice thought. Do it weekly and it compounds."}, {"s": "Thaler & Benartzi, Save More Tomorrow (2004)", "g": "Their field studies asked employees to commit in advance to saving a portion of future raises automatically. Participation and saving rates rose dramatically because the decision was made once, in the cool, and executed without willpower. The finding launched behavioral finance’s automation principle.", "w": "One automated transfer on payday to the debt or the net. The Money Protocol’s floor arrives by removing the monthly decision, not by making it well each month."}, {"s": "Housel, The Psychology of Money (2020)", "g": "Twenty short essays; the core claim is that financial outcomes are driven more by behaviour than by knowledge, and that “enough” is the hardest number to define because the goalpost moves. Security is the gap between what you have and what you need, and most people never examine the second term.", "w": "The Tao’s enough line with charts. Your floor, comfort and enough numbers already exist; his contribution is the warning that the third one will try to move."}, {"s": "Diop; ubuntu", "g": "Cheikh Anta Diop’s project was restoring African historical self-knowledge so that a people could not be defined by another’s story about them. Ubuntu — a person is a person through other persons — locates security in embeddedness rather than fortification.", "w": "The security no institution can revoke is knowing your lineage. The security no bank account delivers is being held. Both are on the thinking path because neither is arithmetic."}],
      trap: 'Working only the arithmetic. The felt security is the one you lack, and it is built by being held, not by being solvent.' },

    { id: 12, cluster: 'C', text: 'Who exactly am I?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Whether identity is a thing to discover, a story to author, or a record to keep. The research favors the second and your own grammar the third; they are compatible.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'McAdams’s narrative identity: the internalized evolving life story, with decades of studies linking story features to wellbeing. Rogers on congruence. Miller’s Drama of the Gifted Child: the child who learns to perform for approval and loses access to what he feels — the clinical description of the shamed dreamer.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Orí: identity as recollection of a chosen destiny. Ubuntu: constituted by relationship. Akan okra and sunsum, the latter shaped by conduct. Mussar: character is the curriculum.'],
        ['History', 'The oríkì tradition: identity as accumulated praise-names earned by deed. Narrative identity with a thousand-year track record.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Numerology and Human Design [S] — see the symbolic lenses. Goffman’s Presentation of Self: the performed self named from the outside.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Narrative identity and wellbeing: McAdams, large literature.', 'A'], ['Congruence (Rogers): supported indirectly by outcome research.', 'B'], ['Miller’s gifted child: clinical, no trials.', 'C']],
      prompts: ['Do not write a definition. McAdams’s Life Story Interview, abbreviated: your life as a book with chapters. Name them. Then the high point, the low point, the turning point, each as a scene.', 'Where in that story were you performing and where not? Miller’s question: when did you learn that being yourself was less safe than being useful?', 'The typology audit: every label you have adopted (Manifestor, 3/5, nature element, dreamer, translator). For each: does it currently lead you to a need, or end the inquiry?', 'The ledger answer: three things you kept this month that cost you something. A truer answer than any label.', 'Ubuntu: who you are to the five people who know you best, one line each. Then who you are to yourself. Compare.', 'If-then: one oríkì entry, earned, this week.'],
      practices: ['lifestory', 'evidencefile', 'labelcheck'],
      read: ['McAdams, The Stories We Live By (1993)', 'Miller, The Drama of the Gifted Child (1979)', 'Goffman, The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life (1956)', 'Rogers, On Becoming a Person (1961)'],
      notes: [{"s": "McAdams, The Stories We Live By (1993)", "g": "McAdams argues identity is a story the person constructs and revises — the internalised, evolving narrative of self — and developed the Life Story Interview to elicit it: chapters, high point, low point, turning point. Decades of studies find that stories with redemption sequences (bad turning to good) and agency predict wellbeing and generativity in midlife.", "w": "Do the interview once, ninety minutes. The chapters you name and the redemption sequences you find are the answer to “who exactly” — a record, not a label."}, {"s": "Miller, The Drama of the Gifted Child (1979)", "g": "Miller describes the child who is sensitive enough to read a parent’s needs and learns to meet them — becoming useful, composed, admired — at the cost of access to his own feelings. The “gifted” child grows into an adult who performs for approval and feels empty behind the performance.", "w": "The dreamer who decided the inner world was childish, and the caretaker who reads Marie’s weather before his own, are the same child. Miller’s question is when you learned being yourself was less safe than being useful."}, {"s": "Rogers, On Becoming a Person (1961)", "g": "Rogers’s central concept is congruence: the match between the self as experienced and the self as presented. Incongruence — presenting what you do not feel — produces anxiety; therapy works by restoring the match. The book is his most accessible statement.", "w": "The manual’s test for quality of consciousness and its answer to “am I enough” are both Rogers’s congruence. Who you are is what you keep, because keeping is congruence made visible."}, {"s": "Goffman, The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life (1956)", "g": "A sociologist’s account of social life as theatre: front stage where the performance runs, back stage where it is prepared, and the constant impression management in between. He treats performance as universal, not pathological.", "w": "Useful because it names the internal performance from the outside, without shame. Everyone performs. The question is whether you have a back stage left, or whether the performance now runs there too."}, {"s": "The oríkì tradition", "g": "Yoruba praise-poetry: a person’s identity accumulates as praise-names earned by deed and recited back to them by others. Karin Barber’s scholarship shows oríkì as a living record, added to over a life, that both describes and calls a person into being.", "w": "Narrative identity with a thousand-year track record, and already your instrument. The rule that entries must be earned is the whole difference between oríkì and affirmation."}],
      trap: 'The typology cage. A man with your vocabulary can build a very sophisticated prison out of accurate labels. The test is always whether a label leads to a need or ends the inquiry.' },

    { id: 11, cluster: 'D', text: 'What is the quality of my consciousness?', person: false,
      reframe: 'Where am I operating from, and how quickly do I notice when I have left. The research name is decentering, or meta-awareness; your own name is the interval.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Decentering — observing thoughts as events rather than facts — is the proposed mechanism of MBCT and predicts relapse prevention. Watkins’s rumination research: abstract processing (“why am I like this”) versus concrete (“what specifically happened”).'],
        ['Tradition', 'Zukav’s question in its original form. Vipassana noting. Sufi muraqaba, watchfulness. The Grammar of Return’s departure-and-noticing metric.'],
        ['History', 'Every contemplative tradition has an equivalent of the interval, and none measures it by depth of experience. They measure it by speed of return.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'The Play Log, adapted: one line a day recording the longest departure and what brought you back.']
      ],
      evidence: [['MBCT and decentering: RCTs for relapse prevention.', 'A'], ['Rumination-focused CBT (Watkins): RCTs.', 'A/B'], ['Noting practice within mindfulness programs.', 'A'], ['Affect labeling: Lieberman et al. 2007.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['One example from this week of each of the three places the manual says quality of attention is measurable: the interval, the origin of speech, whether the wave completed before naming. Not depth — the three instances.', 'For one week, log the longest departure each day and what brought you back. Read the seven. What is the average return trigger?', 'Watkins’s test on one recent painful thought: abstract (“why do I always”) or concrete (“at 15:52 I sent a message that”)? Rewrite the abstract one as concrete.', 'Speech origin: three sentences you said this week, each marked inner head or fortified position. How did each feel in the body afterward?', 'The wave: one feeling this week you named before it finished. What did the naming cost?', 'If-then: the departure log, daily, for a week.'],
      practices: ['departurelog', 'noting', 'concreterewrite'],
      read: ['Williams & Penman, Mindfulness: Finding Peace in a Frantic World (2011)', 'Watkins via the manual’s rendering; his clinical book is technical'],
      notes: [{"s": "Segal, Williams & Teasdale, MBCT; decentering", "g": "MBCT was built to prevent depressive relapse and its randomized trials show it halves relapse risk in people with three or more prior episodes. The proposed mechanism is decentering: the trained capacity to observe a thought as a passing mental event rather than a fact requiring response. Williams and Penman’s book is the eight-week program for the public.", "w": "Quality of consciousness, operationally, is decentering speed — how long a thought runs you before you notice it is a thought. That is your interval, with a trial behind it."}, {"s": "Watkins, rumination-focused CBT", "g": "Edward Watkins’s research distinguishes abstract rumination (“why am I like this?”) from concrete processing (“what specifically happened, and what could I do?”). Abstract processing prolongs low mood and impairs problem-solving; concrete processing does the reverse. His treatment trains the shift, and randomized trials support it.", "w": "The Forge’s Concrete Test — could a camera have recorded it — is Watkins’s core move. When a thought starts with “why do I always,” rewrite it as a timestamp."}, {"s": "Lieberman et al. 2007, affect labeling", "g": "In brain-imaging experiments, putting a feeling into words — simply labeling “angry” or “afraid” while viewing an emotional face — reduced amygdala activity and increased prefrontal regulation. Naming dampens the alarm.", "w": "The wave completing before you name it, and naming it once it has: both matter. The Naming section exists because Lieberman’s finding says the word itself lowers the volume."}, {"s": "Vipassana noting; Sufi muraqaba", "g": "Noting practice labels each mental event by category as it arises — thinking, planning, judging, remembering — without following the content. Muraqaba is the Sufi discipline of watchfulness over the heart’s movements. Both train the observer rather than the observed.", "w": "Five minutes a day of one-word categories. Not the content. The category. The interval shortens because you have practised catching the departure at its first word."}],
      trap: 'Grading depth. The Anti-Performance Clause forbids it. Quality is speed of return, and nothing else is watched.' },

    { id: 14, cluster: 'D', text: 'What need is my energy serving?', person: true,
      reframe: 'What am I actually trying to get when I do what I do, and is the strategy serving the need or impersonating it.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'SDT’s three needs as the tested version of Rosenberg’s list; daily need satisfaction predicts daily wellbeing. NVC as the practice layer: feeling, need, request.'],
        ['Tradition', 'The caretaker as “tending other people’s orí while one’s own goes unfed.” Mussar’s trait inventory as a needs map. Naikan converts “what do I need from them” into “what have I received.”'],
        ['History', 'Over-giving has a genealogy — the eldest son, the provider, the diaspora remittance economy — that makes it look like virtue. Naming it removes the shame that keeps it running.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'The exchange-rate sentence from your own practicum: a person whose word is reliable currency no longer has to purchase belonging with caretaking. A hypothesis; the ledger tests it.']
      ],
      evidence: [['SDT need satisfaction.', 'A'], ['NVC training: few controlled studies, modest effects; large practitioner base.', 'B/C'], ['Naikan.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['For one week, at day’s end: the three most energy-consuming things you did and the need each served. Rosenberg’s list or SDT’s three.', 'Then: was that a need, or a strategy for a need you have not named? Over-giving is a strategy. Under it is usually belonging or being chosen.', 'Naikan on Marie for one week: received, given, trouble caused. Where does the ledger surprise you?', 'The four unnamed needs the manual names — chosen, safety, worth, belonging. For each: the strategy you currently use, and what a direct request would sound like.', 'Third-person reread: what does Bate seem to believe he must do to be allowed to belong?', 'If-then: one direct request this week, in NVC form, where a strategy would have run.'],
      practices: ['needlog', 'directrequest', 'naikan'],
      read: ['Rosenberg — in the manual', 'Pink, Drive (2009)', 'Reynolds, Flowing Bridges, Quiet Waters (1989)'],
      notes: [{"s": "Rosenberg, Nonviolent Communication", "g": "Rosenberg’s model holds that every action is an attempt to meet a universal human need, and conflict arises from strategies, never from needs. His four-step form — observation, feeling, need, request — replaces judgment with disclosure. Controlled studies are few and modest; the practitioner base is large.", "w": "The question in your pocket is his: what need is this serving? Then the sharper one — is this a need, or a strategy for a need I have not said aloud?"}, {"s": "Self-Determination Theory, need satisfaction", "g": "Deci and Ryan’s three needs — autonomy, competence, relatedness — are the empirically tested subset of Rosenberg’s longer list. Daily-diary research finds that satisfaction of each predicts that day’s wellbeing, and thwarting any one predicts distress even when the others are met.", "w": "When the need log fills with “belonging” every day and never “autonomy,” the pattern is telling you which need the over-giving is buying, and at what cost to the other two."}, {"s": "Naikan; Reynolds, Flowing Bridges, Quiet Waters (1989)", "g": "Yoshimoto’s method asks three questions about a specific person: what I received, what I gave, what trouble I caused. The fourth — what trouble they caused me — is excluded because it is the question everyone already runs. Used clinically in Japan and China; randomized trials, mostly Chinese, show modest benefit as an adjunct. Reynolds’s book presents it for Western readers alongside Morita.", "w": "The caretaker’s ledger records only what he gave. Naikan on Marie for a week forces the other column open, and the result is usually a surprise in your favour."}],
      trap: 'Analyzing needs instead of requesting them. This question produces beautiful inventories. The only evidence it worked is a request made out loud.' },

    { id: 16, cluster: 'D', text: 'How do I meet my wounds?', person: false, routing: true,
      reframe: 'The difference between understanding a wound and meeting one, and what container makes meeting safe. The question where the routing rule matters most.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Pennebaker’s expressive writing as the tested version of “meeting.” Rituals reduce grief in controlled experiments (Norton & Gino 2014) — direct support for the Two Waters as container. IFS “parts” language as a reflective lens only; thin evidence and a documented destabilization risk when protective parts are bypassed. Brach’s RAIN.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Somé: unwept grief hardens and blocks the flow of life; the Dagara grief ritual is communal for a reason. The salt-water release. Shiva as structured, time-bound, witnessed grief. “The sentences may be broken.”'],
        ['History', 'Modern Western culture is the first to make grief private and unscheduled. Nearly every other built a container with a start, a witness, and an end. The Two Waters is a solo reconstruction of that architecture.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Unsent letters — to the person, the wound, the younger self. Narrative therapy; overlaps with expressive writing in evidence.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Expressive writing: meta-analyses.', 'A'], ['Ritual and grief: Norton & Gino 2014; Hobson et al. 2018.', 'B'], ['IFS: a small number of trials, pilots, 2025 scoping review; destabilization risk documented.', 'C'], ['Somatic approaches: Brom et al. 2017.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['The difference between understanding a wound and meeting one, in your own words. Which do you usually do?', 'The wounds you know about — the ones that still run, not the whole history. For each: has it ever been witnessed by another person?', 'Pennebaker’s protocol on one wound: twenty minutes, four consecutive days, no editing. Day five, reread in third person.', 'RAIN on one feeling that arose: recognize it, allow it, investigate where it lives in the body, nurture it with one sentence you would say to a child.', 'The routing check: did any of this frighten you about yourself, or stay with you for days in a way you could not put down? If yes, Routing applies before any further session on this question.', 'If-then: one Two Waters session on the wound, container kept — invocation, work, closure — this week.'],
      practices: ['expressive', 'twowaters', 'unsentletter'],
      read: ['Pennebaker & Smyth, Opening Up by Writing It Down (2016)', 'Brach, Radical Acceptance (2003)', 'Somé, Of Water and the Spirit — in the manual', 'Schwartz, No Bad Parts (2021) — vocabulary only'],
      notes: [{"s": "Pennebaker & Smyth, Opening Up by Writing It Down (2016)", "g": "Since 1986 Pennebaker’s protocol — write about your deepest thoughts and feelings on a troubling experience for fifteen to twenty minutes, three or four consecutive days, no editing — has been tested in over a hundred studies with reliable modest gains in immune function, doctor visits, mood and working memory. The proposed mechanism: writing imposes structure and narrative on experience, which rumination never provides, and the cost of inhibition is released.", "w": "Meeting a wound, tested: four days, twenty minutes, one wound, no editing. Then reread in third person. The Two Waters gives it a container; Pennebaker gives it a dose."}, {"s": "Norton & Gino 2014, rituals and grief", "g": "Across experiments, people who performed a ritual after a loss — even an invented one — reported less grief and a greater sense of control than those who did not, and the effect held for the loss of a lover, a loved one, or a lottery. The ritual’s content did not matter; performing it did.", "w": "The Two Waters’ invocation-work-closure is not decoration. The research says the frame is what makes intensity safe, and that even a solo, self-built rite works."}, {"s": "Brach, Radical Acceptance (2003); RAIN", "g": "Tara Brach’s RAIN sequence — Recognize what is happening; Allow it to be there; Investigate with kindness, especially in the body; Nurture with compassion — is a mindfulness structure for meeting a difficult feeling without fleeing or drowning. Her book frames the alternative as the trance of unworthiness.", "w": "After the writing surfaces something, RAIN is how you stay with it for two minutes instead of explaining it for twenty."}, {"s": "Schwartz, No Bad Parts (2021); IFS", "g": "Internal Family Systems describes the psyche as parts — exiles carrying wounds, managers and firefighters protecting against them — around a core Self. The vocabulary is useful; the evidence is two small RCTs and a 2025 scoping review calling it promising, and clinicians document destabilization when protectors are bypassed too fast without a therapist.", "w": "Read it for the language of parts. Do not run it on yourself as treatment. The Aloof and the caretaker are protectors in his terms, and protectors are there for a reason."}, {"s": "Somé, Of Water and the Spirit (1994)", "g": "Somé’s account of Dagara life includes communal grief ritual: grief is wept out loud, in company, in a container with a start and an end, because unwept grief hardens and blocks the flow of life. Grief held alone is understood as dangerous to the person and the village.", "w": "The witness is not optional past a certain weight. Your Two Waters is a solo reconstruction; the routing rule is where solo stops."}],
      trap: 'Solo depth as bravery. Pursued alone, depth is not braver, only uncontained. The witness is not optional past a certain weight.' },

    { id: 2, cluster: 'E', text: 'What is true masculinity?', person: true,
      reframe: 'What kind of man is worth becoming, and what it costs. “True” as in plumb and load-bearing, not essential.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'Real’s covert depression in men: disconnection from feeling as the male inheritance, repair through relational presence. APA 2018: rigid masculine norms — stoicism, self-reliance, dominance — predict worse mental and physical health.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Ìwà pẹ̀lẹ́ as the precondition of authority. Lorde on protective versus punitive force. Baldwin on the mask. hooks, The Will to Change.'],
        ['History', 'Kimmel’s Manhood in America for the breadwinner script. Somé and van Gennep on initiation. The Cross River Ekpe/Ngbe leopard society as a documented graded men’s institution in your own geography — verify with elders before claiming it.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'Men’s groups: observational studies show wellbeing gains; no RCTs. The mechanism, when it works, is witnessed vulnerability among men — the thing the amputation removed.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Rigid masculine norms and health: large survey literature; APA 2018.', 'A'], ['Men’s group participation: observational.', 'C'], ['Real’s clinical model: decades of use, no trials.', 'C']],
      prompts: ['Your own definition from the questionnaire — conscious, emotionally alive, respects women, worth not tied to stereotypes. For each clause: one man you know who embodies it, and one moment this month where you did not.', 'Real’s question: where does your own disconnection from feeling show up? The last time you answered “I’m fine” when it was false.', 'The four testable properties — keeps his word, holds another’s weather, protects without dominating, can be seen. Write one line of evidence for each, this month.', 'Interview one man about his father. What was passed down, what was withheld. Fieldwork, not reflection — then a written debrief: what was said, in three lines.', 'If-then: one conversation with a man this week that goes past “how are you.”'],
      practices: ['mancall', 'fatherinterview'],
      read: ['Real, I Don’t Want to Talk About It (1997)', 'hooks, The Will to Change — in the manual', 'Kimmel, Manhood in America (1996)'],
      notes: [{"s": "Real, I Don’t Want to Talk About It (1997)", "g": "Real, a family therapist, describes covert depression in men: not sadness but disconnection — from feeling, from others — masked by work, anger, drink or withdrawal, and passed father to son. His treatment restores relational presence: the man learns to feel and to say it in the room with the people it concerns.", "w": "“I’m fine” when it is false is his diagnostic sentence. The Aloof drama is covert depression with better manners, and his repair — say it in the room — is the exposure ladder in a therapist’s language."}, {"s": "APA Guidelines for Psychological Practice with Boys and Men (2018)", "g": "A review of decades of research concluding that rigid adherence to traditional masculine norms — stoicism, self-reliance, dominance, avoidance of vulnerability — is associated with worse mental and physical health, lower help-seeking and higher risk-taking. The association is robust; the norms are learned, not innate.", "w": "The four testable properties in the manual are the inverse of the norms this review indicts. Keeps his word, holds weather, protects without dominating, can be seen."}, {"s": "hooks, The Will to Change (2004)", "g": "hooks argues that patriarchy wounds boys first by requiring the amputation of feeling, and that most men never learn there was an operation. Her book is addressed to men as an invitation to love and be loved, and to women as an account of why the men they love are absent.", "w": "Your own definition — conscious, emotionally alive, respects women, worth not tied to stereotypes — is hooks’s in your words. The open question is whether you are it on the hard days."}, {"s": "Kimmel, Manhood in America (1996)", "g": "A cultural history tracing American manhood from the self-made man of the early republic through the breadwinner ideal of industrialization to the anxious performance of the twentieth century. His thesis: manhood has always been proved to other men, never settled, which makes it a perpetual audition.", "w": "The room you want to build is an attempt to end the audition. Kimmel’s history says no generation has yet managed it, which is not an argument against trying."}, {"s": "Men’s groups; Ekpe/Ngbe society", "g": "Observational studies of men’s groups such as the ManKind Project report wellbeing gains; no randomized trials exist. The mechanism, where it works, is witnessed vulnerability among men. The Cross River Ekpe (leopard) society of southwest Cameroon and southeast Nigeria was a graded men’s institution with its own script, Nsibidi — a historical model of structured male initiation in your geography, to be verified against your lineage with elders.", "w": "The question is a syllabus. Answered alone it becomes another framework; answered in a room of men it becomes the room."}],
      trap: 'Defining masculinity in a room alone. The question is a syllabus for a community; answered solo it becomes another framework.' },

    { id: 3, cluster: 'E', text: 'How do mind and speech relate to masculinity?', person: true,
      reframe: 'Whether the intellect is an instrument or a bunker, and whether speech is withheld, wielded, or offered. The question where your gift and your armor share a substance.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'hooks on the sanctioned faculty: boys taught intellect is safe and feeling is soft. Rosenberg’s jackal and giraffe. Affect labeling: naming feelings aloud reduces their intensity — speech is where regulation happens, not only where masculinity is audited.'],
        ['Tradition', 'The conduction sequence: orí, then ìwà, then àṣẹ. Speech that runs the sequence carries authority; speech that skips to utterance is armor. Lorde on silence. Epictetus on the tongue.'],
        ['History', 'Oral cultures treated speech as consequential act, not commentary. The internal performance is a written-culture disease: a mind that learned to narrate rather than to speak.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'The clarity test: is there a defendant in this sentence? Improv’s “yes, and” as a speech discipline that forbids the case.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Affect labeling: Lieberman 2007 and replications.', 'B'], ['NVC.', 'B/C'], ['Improv and communication: Felsman.', 'B']],
      prompts: ['Three arguments you won this month. For each: what did winning cost the other person, and what did it cost you?', 'Three silences this month that were withholding rather than rest. What did each protect?', 'Run the conduction sequence on one thing you want to say to Marie: is it mine to say (orí)? Can I say it without armor (ìwà)? Then write it as you would say it (àṣẹ).', 'The clarity test on your last five messages to anyone: count the defendants.', 'If-then: one thing said this week from the inner head, without the case.'],
      practices: ['pause3', 'affectlabel', 'defendantcount'],
      read: ['Rosenberg', 'hooks', 'Epictetus, Enchiridion 33 and 41 — two pages'],
      notes: [{"s": "hooks, on the sanctioned faculty", "g": "hooks describes how boys learn that intellect is safe and feeling is soft: the mind becomes the only permitted instrument, and it is trained to defend rather than to connect. The result is a man who can explain everything and feel nothing he can name.", "w": "The case built mid-conversation is real intelligence deployed as armor. Your gift and your defense are the same material, which is why this question is yours."}, {"s": "Lieberman et al. 2007", "g": "Naming a feeling aloud reduced amygdala activity in brain-imaging experiments. Speech is not only where masculinity is audited; it is where regulation physically happens.", "w": "“I’m anxious about the interview,” said to someone, out loud, is a regulation technique with a scan behind it. Not processed. Named."}, {"s": "Rosenberg, jackal and giraffe", "g": "Rosenberg’s teaching image: jackal speech judges, diagnoses and demands; giraffe speech (the animal with the largest heart) observes, feels, names a need and requests. He treats eloquence in the service of judgment as violence with good grammar.", "w": "The perfectly reasonable sentence that leaves her nowhere to stand is jackal in a suit. The conduction sequence — orí, ìwà, àṣẹ — is the manual’s giraffe."}, {"s": "Epictetus, Enchiridion 33 and 41", "g": "Section 33 prescribes silence as the default, speaking briefly when required, avoiding talk about people and events, never laughing loudly, never swearing. Section 41 says spending time on the body is a mark of a small mind — attention belongs on the mind. Two pages on the discipline of the tongue.", "w": "The three-second pause before a consequential sentence is Stoic practice. The pause is where the sequence runs and the case fails to assemble."}, {"s": "Felsman, improv", "g": "Improv training reduced social anxiety and raised uncertainty tolerance in controlled studies. Its speech rule — yes, and — forbids blocking, correcting, or winning, which makes it a discipline against the case.", "w": "Eloquence wins the argument and loses the room. Improv is speech practice in which winning is against the rules."}],
      trap: 'Eloquence. You have it, and it cannot be what earns the room. The practice is congruence repeated, and it sounds plainer than you like.' },

    { id: 17, cluster: 'E', text: 'What is the inherited history of men?', person: true, routing: true,
      reframe: 'What did I receive without choosing, from which sources, and which of it do I keep. An audit, and audits require documents.',
      lenses: [
        ['Psychology', 'hooks’s amputation. Real’s covert depression as the transmitted form. Intergenerational transmission of attachment: parents’ attachment states predict children’s.'],
        ['Tradition', 'Somé on the uninitiated man who overcompensates. Van Gennep’s three stages — separation, liminality, incorporation — and Turner on liminality. The Cross River Ekpe/Ngbe societies as graded initiation in your region, to be verified with elders.'],
        ['History', 'The breadwinner script is roughly 150 years old (Griswold, Rotundo, Blankenhorn on the industrial reorganization of fatherhood). Fanon on the colonial wound to manhood. The manual holds the four-layer account; this adds the instruction to check it against your own family.'],
        ['Unconventional', 'The genogram (Bowen, McGoldrick): a three-generation map with patterns marked — who provided, who left, who drank, who was silent. Drawing it produces insight that reading about inheritance does not.']
      ],
      evidence: [['Intergenerational transmission of attachment: meta-analyses.', 'A'], ['Masculine norms and outcomes.', 'A'], ['Genogram: clinical tool, decades of use, no trials.', 'C']],
      prompts: ['Draw the genogram: three generations, both sides. Mark providers, absentees, the silent, the violent, the tender, the ones who left, the ones who stayed. Two pages — then a written debrief: what was said, in three lines.', 'For each man on it, one line: what he passed down, what he withheld.', 'Your keep-and-burn list — keep respect for elders, family, living history; burn widow persecution, male impunity, women as property. For each burn item: the mechanism by which it was transmitted to you. Usually silence.', 'Interview one elder about initiation: what marked the passage from boy to man in their time, and who witnessed it. If nothing, that is the finding — then a written debrief: what was said, in three lines.', 'What your son will inherit from you if nothing changes. Then the one item you would change first.', 'If-then: the elder interview, this month.'],
      practices: ['genogram', 'elderinterview', 'burnlist'],
      read: ['Kimmel, Manhood in America', 'Real', 'McGoldrick, Genograms — first chapter is enough', 'Van Gennep, The Rites of Passage (1909) — introduction and chapter one', 'Somé — in the manual'],
      notes: [{"s": "hooks, the amputation; Real, transmission", "g": "hooks: the first act of a boy’s gendering is a psychic self-surgery cutting away tenderness and fear. Real: covert depression passes from father to son as a way of being that the son does not know he learned.", "w": "What you inherited without choosing has a mechanism, and the mechanism is usually silence. Naming it in company is how an inheritance stops transmitting."}, {"s": "Intergenerational transmission of attachment", "g": "Meta-analyses find that a parent’s attachment state, measured before a child is born, predicts the infant’s attachment classification with moderate reliability. Security and insecurity are passed down through the quality of attunement, not through genes alone — and the pattern can be broken by a parent who has made sense of their own history.", "w": "Your son inherits the room you were in. The research adds the hopeful clause: a father who has reflected on his own inheritance transmits differently."}, {"s": "Griswold, Rotundo, Blankenhorn — the breadwinner script", "g": "Historians of fatherhood find that pre-industrial fathers were primary moral educators of children; industrialization separated home from work and produced the male breadwinner as the marker of manhood by the mid-nineteenth century. Rotundo dates the turn to “passionate manhood” — bold action over reflection — to the 1880s and 1890s. The equation of manhood with earning is roughly 150 years old.", "w": "It feels eternal. It is a phase of industrial capitalism. Financial insecurity lands as existential insecurity because a recent script told you they were the same thing."}, {"s": "Fanon, the colonial wound", "g": "Fanon documents what subjugation does to a colonised man’s sense of his own manhood and the compensations that follow: hardness, control asserted where power was stripped, and displacement of thwarted power onto women and children in the household.", "w": "Overlay this on the breadwinner script and you get the inheritance you named for the burn list: widow persecution, male impunity, women as property. Each has a mechanism, and Fanon names the wound under it."}, {"s": "Somé; van Gennep, The Rites of Passage (1909); Turner", "g": "Van Gennep identified the three-stage structure of every passage rite — separation, liminality, incorporation — and Turner developed liminality as the threshold state where the old identity is gone and the new not yet conferred. Somé’s account of Dagara initiation shows boys becoming men through witnessed passage held by elders, and describes the uninitiated man as one who performs manhood because no one confirmed he arrived.", "w": "The missing eldership is the loss under the others. The elder interview asks what marked the passage in their time; if the answer is nothing, that is the finding, and the room you build is the response."}, {"s": "McGoldrick, Genograms", "g": "The genogram is family therapy’s three-generation map with standardised symbols for relationships, cutoffs, illness, addiction and patterns. McGoldrick’s point is that drawing it produces recognitions that talking about family does not — patterns become visible across generations on a single page.", "w": "Reading the four-layer history is general. Drawing your own is specific, and where the drawing contradicts the general account is where your curriculum is."}],
      trap: 'Reading the history instead of drawing your own. The four-layer account is general. The genogram is yours, and where it contradicts the general account is where the real curriculum is.' }
  ],
  practices: {
    ifthen: { name: 'The if-then week', grade: 'A', freq: 'Weekly, Sunday', min: 2, record: 'One implementation intention, trigger named, in the ledger.', mapsTo: 'The àfọ̀ṣẹ sentence / Kept Word' },
    fivemin: { name: 'The five-minute start', grade: 'A', freq: 'When stuck', min: 5, record: 'Set a timer. Stop at five if you want. Five minutes cannot be perfect.', mapsTo: 'Behavioral activation' },
    arugamama: { name: 'Arugamama', grade: 'B', freq: 'When fear of starting arises', min: 1, record: 'Name it in one line (“fear of the first bad page is here”), then the next physical act — with the fear present, not after.', mapsTo: 'Morita' },
    sleepanchor: { name: 'The sleep anchor', grade: 'A', freq: 'Daily', min: 0, record: 'One fixed wake time, seven days a week, before any other sleep intervention. Not eight hours. One time.', mapsTo: 'CBT-I, first move' },
    greenhour: { name: 'The green hour', grade: 'A/B', freq: 'Daily', min: 20, record: 'Off pavement, among living things. Ravine, lakeshore, any wild edge. Intake, not exercise.', mapsTo: 'Nature exposure, 120 min/week' },
    refusal: { name: 'One refusal a week', grade: 'A', freq: 'Weekly', min: 1, record: 'One “no” where the caretaker would have said yes. Written with what it cost. The guilt afterward is the sound of the pattern losing.', mapsTo: 'Assertiveness training' },
    urgesurf: { name: 'Urge surfing', grade: 'A', freq: 'When the reach begins', min: 2, record: 'Do not fight it. Notice where it lives in the body, breathe, watch it crest and fall over ninety seconds.', mapsTo: 'Marlatt, relapse prevention' },
    oneword: { name: 'The one-word log', grade: 'B', freq: 'Three days', min: 0, record: 'One word per phone reach, before the reach. Affect labeling at the moment it matters.', mapsTo: 'Lieberman 2007' },
    cutoff: { name: 'The 9:30 cutoff', grade: 'B', freq: 'Nightly', min: 0, record: 'Already running. Phone charges outside the bedroom; grayscale after 9:00.', mapsTo: 'Digital friction' },
    morningminute: { name: 'The morning minute', grade: 'C', freq: 'Daily', min: 1, record: 'Same practice as Omi tútù / Morning Anchor. Before the phone: three breaths, one sentence of intention. Sixty seconds. This is the choose.', mapsTo: 'Omi tútù / Morning Anchor' },
    eveningreview: { name: 'The evening review', grade: 'C', freq: 'Daily', min: 5, record: 'Same practice as The Zengzi Close. One thing received; one moment toward what matters; one away; one line on what tomorrow asks; close. Examen, muhasaba and the Stoic review in their shortest shared form.', mapsTo: 'The Zengzi Close' },
    peacemap: { name: 'The peace map', grade: 'C', freq: 'One week', min: 1, record: 'At day’s end mark where any peace occurred: body, water, green, work, Marie, alone-withdrawn, alone-sanctuary. Seven days gives a map.', mapsTo: null },
    sigh: { name: 'Cyclic sighing', grade: 'A', freq: 'Daily, and before every session', min: 5, record: 'Same practice as Return-in-Place. Nose in, second short inhale, long mouth exhale. Five minutes. The floor under everything else.', mapsTo: 'Return-in-Place' },
    hourlydrop: { name: 'The hourly drop', grade: 'B', freq: 'Hourly, one day', min: 0, record: 'The question, hourly, one word — the body part that answers first.', mapsTo: 'Interoception training' },
    focusing: { name: 'Focusing, short form', grade: 'B', freq: 'Weekly', min: 10, record: 'Clear a space; feel the whole problem in the body; find a word or image that fits; check it against the body; let it be.', mapsTo: 'Gendlin' },
    rehab: { name: 'The rehab', grade: 'A', freq: 'Once', min: 60, record: 'The ankles and knees. A physiotherapist, once. The body has been asking.', mapsTo: null },
    ladder: { name: 'The visibility ladder', grade: 'A', freq: 'One rung a week or faster', min: 0, record: 'Ten rungs, easiest to hardest, each an act of being seen unpolished. Predicted discomfort 1–10 before, actual after. The gap is the lesson.', mapsTo: 'Exposure' },
    preritual: { name: 'The pre-performance ritual', grade: 'B', freq: 'Before any rung', min: 1, record: 'Two long exhales, feet on the floor, one line. Arbitrary is fine. Consistent is what works.', mapsTo: 'Brooks et al. 2016' },
    improv: { name: 'Improv or Toastmasters', grade: 'B/C', freq: 'Once, as rung five or six', min: 90, record: 'One class or one meeting. Not to join. To test the rung — then a written debrief: what was said, in three lines.', mapsTo: 'Felsman et al.' },
    selfcompassion: { name: 'The self-compassion break', grade: 'A', freq: 'When the question fires', min: 1, record: 'Hand on chest, three sentences: this is a moment of suffering; suffering is part of being human; may I be kind to myself in this moment. Absurd for a week, then not.', mapsTo: 'Neff' },
    evidencefile: { name: 'The evidence file', grade: 'A/B', freq: 'Weekly', min: 2, record: 'Same practice as The Oríkì file, run as a positive data log. One line, deed-anchored, earned only. Read it when the tribunal sits.', mapsTo: 'The Oríkì file, run as a positive data log' },
    rename: { name: 'Kross’s rename', grade: 'B', freq: 'When the loop starts', min: 0, record: '“Bate is asking whether he is enough again. What happened just before?” The third person has no defendant.', mapsTo: 'Self-distancing' },
    valuessort: { name: 'The values card sort', grade: 'A/B', freq: 'Once', min: 10, record: 'The ACT values list sorted very important / important / less. Keep the top five visible.', mapsTo: 'ACT' },
    withoutnod: { name: 'One act without the nod', grade: 'A', freq: 'Weekly', min: 0, record: 'Something done as if the audience did not exist. Logged with predicted and actual reaction.', mapsTo: 'Behavioral experiment' },
    publicsolitude: { name: 'Public solitude', grade: 'C', freq: 'Weekly', min: 5, record: 'In any public setting, five minutes of full attention on one task, as if alone. The stage skill, trained cheaply.', mapsTo: 'Stanislavski' },
    automation: { name: 'Automation', grade: 'A', freq: 'Once, then payday', min: 10, record: 'One transfer, automated, on payday, to the debt or the net. Removes the decision.', mapsTo: 'Save More Tomorrow' },
    securityprime: { name: 'Security priming', grade: 'B', freq: 'Weekly', min: 2, record: 'Recall one of the three safe moments in detail, in the body.', mapsTo: 'Mikulincer & Shaver' },
    holdingcall: { name: 'The holding call', grade: 'C', freq: 'Weekly', min: 20, record: 'One call to a man who holds you. Boris, Laurent-Olivier, Glenn. Not to report. To be held.', mapsTo: 'Ubuntu' },
    lifestory: { name: 'The life story chapters', grade: 'A', freq: 'Once; reread annually', min: 90, record: 'Chapters, scenes, and the redemption sequences where bad became good.', mapsTo: 'McAdams' },
    labelcheck: { name: 'The label check', grade: 'C', freq: 'Monthly', min: 5, record: 'Does each label I use lead me to a need this month, or end a question? Retire any that only end questions.', mapsTo: null },
    departurelog: { name: 'The departure log', grade: 'B', freq: 'Daily', min: 1, record: 'One line: longest departure, return trigger. Read weekly. The interval, measured.', mapsTo: 'Meta-awareness' },
    noting: { name: 'Noting', grade: 'A', freq: 'Daily', min: 5, record: 'Sit, and label each mental event with one word as it arises. Not the content. The category.', mapsTo: 'Vipassana / MBCT' },
    concreterewrite: { name: 'Concrete rewrite', grade: 'A/B', freq: 'When an abstract thought appears', min: 1, record: 'Rewrite it as a camera would record it.', mapsTo: 'Watkins / the Forge’s Concrete Test' },
    needlog: { name: 'The need log', grade: 'A/B', freq: 'Daily', min: 1, record: 'One line: the biggest energy spend and its need. Weekly review.', mapsTo: 'SDT' },
    directrequest: { name: 'The direct request', grade: 'B/C', freq: 'Weekly', min: 0, record: 'A request made in feeling-need-request form where the caretaker would have simply provided. Logged.', mapsTo: 'NVC' },
    naikan: { name: 'Naikan on one person', grade: 'B', freq: 'Weekly', min: 5, record: 'Same practice as Yoshimoto. Received, given, trouble caused. Rotate the person.', mapsTo: 'Yoshimoto' },
    expressive: { name: 'Expressive writing, four days', grade: 'A', freq: 'Four consecutive days', min: 20, record: 'On one wound. Twenty minutes, no editing, then close.', mapsTo: 'Pennebaker' },
    twowaters: { name: 'Two Waters', grade: 'B', freq: 'Weekly', min: 30, record: 'Same practice as The Waters. With the container intact — invocation, work, closure. The frame is what makes intensity safe.', mapsTo: 'The Waters' },
    unsentletter: { name: 'The unsent letter', grade: 'B', freq: 'When a wound is named', min: 20, record: 'To the person, the wound, or the younger self. Written, read aloud once, kept or released.', mapsTo: 'Narrative therapy' },
    mancall: { name: 'The weekly man call', grade: 'C', freq: 'Weekly', min: 20, record: 'One structured call with one of the named men. What is heavy, what is good, what do you need. Rotate.', mapsTo: null },
    fatherinterview: { name: 'The father interview', grade: 'C', freq: 'Once', min: 45, record: 'Any man, about his father. Material for the community’s first module.', mapsTo: null },
    pause3: { name: 'The three-second pause', grade: 'C', freq: 'Before any consequential sentence', min: 0, record: 'One breath. The pause is where the sequence runs.', mapsTo: 'Epictetus' },
    affectlabel: { name: 'Affect labeling out loud', grade: 'B', freq: 'Daily', min: 0, record: 'Say a feeling aloud to someone: “I’m anxious about the interview.” Not processed. Named.', mapsTo: 'Lieberman 2007' },
    defendantcount: { name: 'The defendant count', grade: 'C', freq: 'Weekly', min: 5, record: 'Reread your messages; count sentences with a defendant. Watch the number fall.', mapsTo: 'The clarity test' },
    genogram: { name: 'The genogram', grade: 'C', freq: 'Once; kept', min: 60, record: 'Three generations, both sides, patterns marked. Reread when the community’s curriculum is written.', mapsTo: 'Bowen, McGoldrick' },
    elderinterview: { name: 'The elder interview', grade: 'C', freq: 'Quarterly', min: 45, record: 'Recorded, with permission. The archive the community will need.', mapsTo: null },
    burnlist: { name: 'The burn-list mechanism', grade: 'C', freq: 'Once per item', min: 10, record: 'For each inherited item you intend to end: its transmission mechanism, and one act that interrupts it.', mapsTo: null }
  },
  symbolic: {
    title: 'The Symbolic Lenses',
    barnum: 'Numerology, Human Design, astrology, tarot and their relatives have no predictive validity in controlled tests. Their descriptions feel accurate because of the Barnum effect: statements general enough to fit most people are experienced as personally accurate. That is not a reason to discard them. It is a reason to use them for what they are good at — generating prompts. A statement that feels accurate is data about what you believe about yourself. One that feels wrong is data too. The systems are mirrors that talk; the value is in your reaction, never in their claim.',
    numerology: 'Pythagorean method. Life path: write the birth date as digits (month, day, year), sum all digits, reduce to a single digit — except 11, 22 and 33 are kept as master numbers. Example: 15 March 1993 is 1+5+3+1+9+9+3 = 31, and 3+1 = 4. Expression number: letters A–Z valued 1–9 repeating (A=1 … I=9, J=1 …), sum the full birth name, reduce. Traditional meanings: 1 initiator; 2 partner; 3 expresser; 4 builder; 5 freedom-seeker; 6 caretaker; 7 seeker and analyst; 8 power and provision; 9 completer; 11 intuitive; 22 master builder; 33 master teacher. Compute both. Read the description. Write what resonated, what did not, and why. Then write which resonance you would have predicted from your own self-image — that last step is the whole exercise. Decide nothing from it.',
    hd: 'The Architecture holds the design. Same rule: Manifestor, 3/5, emotional authority are prompt generators, and the Q12 test governs them — does the label lead to a need, or end the inquiry?',
    rule: 'No symbolic system enters the ledger. The ledger records kept words, departures and requests. Nothing symbolic is ever a reason for or against an action recorded there.'
  },
  routing: {
    title: 'Routing — when the container is too small',
    intro: 'Q16 and parts of Q5 and Q17 can reach material solo work should not hold. Stop the session and route to a professional if any of the following occur:',
    signs: ['Material from a session stays with you for days in a way you cannot put down.', 'Sleep drops below four hours for more than a week, or eating stops.', 'Any thought of harming yourself — even fleeting, even “just a thought.”', 'Anything you learn about yourself that frightens you.', 'Grief that does not move after the container closes.'],
    options: 'In Ontario: a registered psychotherapist or psychologist (many offer sliding scale; Psychology Today’s directory filters by fee); the family doctor for referral; Ontario Structured Psychotherapy for CBT at no cost, by referral. A therapist trained in CBT, ACT, MBCT, expressive writing or somatic approaches will recognize everything here.',
    close: 'This is not the practice failing. It is the practice being honest about its scope — a sentence you wrote yourself.'
  }
};
